e votes of confidence last month  showed that a large number of our  colleagues agree it was a moment for  humility grip and a new direction i  regret to say this is the key bit i  regret to say however that it is clear  to me that this situation will not  change under your leadership and you  have therefore lost my confidence too so  that is obviously a very very personal  attack uh on the prime minister wounding  him uh very deeply and then  we have the letter from sajid that's  from uh rishi sunak sorry the the  chancellor of the exchequer who says  the public rightly expect government to  be conducted properly competently and  seriously i recognize this maybe my last  ministerial job but i believe these  standards are worth fighting for and  that's why i'm resigning i'm sad to be  leaving the government but i've  reluctantly come to the conclusion that  we cannot continue like this both of  these ministers basically pointing the  finger at boris johnson saying that he  is the problem it's his personality it's  his leadership that is costing the  conservative party that is eroding trust  in the government and that's why they  have decided to leave a very very  serious blow for from for the prime  minister tonight  rory and this appears to be the  cumulative effect of scandal after  scandal i probably internationally our  audience knows about partygate but there  have been some dubious appointments uh  to senior posts within government  recently and the loss of two  by-elections recently talk us through  boris johnson's bumpy road over the past  couple of years  yeah well part again is the most serious  it's the one uh that has uh caused the  most headlines and has perhaps done most  to erode public trust in this government  and in boris johnson essentially boris  johnson was fined by the police uh along  with more than 100 of his aides and  civil servants  for partying uh through lockdown  at a time when he personally was telling  people around the country that they  should be staying at home they should be  not seeing loved ones  they could not even go into  old people's homes and say goodbye to  grandmothers grandfathers brothers and  fathers who who were perhaps dying um  they couldn't go and say goodbye to  people in hospitals  this was deeply deeply traumatic for the  country and uh to to find out that the  prime minister himself had been going to  parties while all that was going on  perhaps has done more than anything else  to damage his reputation but of course  there are other things as well there was  the owen patterson affair which really  poisoned relations i think with many of  his uh back benches now he was a former  tory minister who was found by the  parliament's standards watchdog to have  committed an egregious breach of  lobbying rules boris johnson then tried  to change those rules to protect  his friend and there was a backlash  which forced him to climb down there was  another scandal involving him decorating  his flats and who paid for that uh  whether that was a kind of cash uh for  favors sort of arrangement and most  recently uh the affair over chris  pincher which has been uh blowing up  this week uh  an mp a deputy whip who was found to  have committed uh  sexual misconduct  downing street said that boris johnson  didn't know anything about this it then  transparent transpired that he did and  earlier on today he came out and was  forced to apologize for yet another  of his  seeming gaffes or crisis let's listen to  how boris johnson put that earlier on  there's no place in this government for  anybody who  is predatory or who  abuses their position of power did you  want to joke though pincher by name  pinscher by nature well what i can tell  you is that uh  if i look at the background of this and  why i regret it so much is that uh  about three years ago uh there was a  complaint made against uh chris pincher  in the foreign office uh the complaint  was was uh  cleared up he apologized uh it was  raised with me  uh in uh orally there was a i was i was  briefed on what had  had happened and  you know if i had my time again  i would think back on it  and uh rory boris johnson seems to have  turned surviving political scandal into  something of an art form uh hasn't he in  the last few minutes or so  we've seen on social media confirmation  from liz trust the foreign secretary  that she will stand firmly behind the  prime minister kwasi kwateng the  business secretary and pride home  secretary and ben wallace defense  secretary all saying that they'll stand  by boris johnson there is still a chance  that he could survive all this  yeah i'll add jacob rees-mogg to that as  well uh i'll read you a  a quote from him the prime minister won  a mandate from the british people and  that is more powerful than cabinet  ministers resigning so clearly he does  still have supporters  but  the question is what he does now uh can  he rally those supporters will they be  enough to keep him in his position  will there be more resignations if there  are other big fish from within uh the  cabinet that who resign then that will  heap more pressure on him  will  there be a renewed attempt by back  benchers and those disenchanted with  boris johnson's leadership within the  conservative party to try to force  another vote of confidence the rules of  the game as set by the 1922 uh committee  are that once you have had a vote of no  confidence against you which he had very  recently and survived there cannot be  another one for a year but those rules  are changeable and  if the 1922 committee decides that it  wants to change the rules they can do so  they can reduce that statute of  limitations so there is a mechanism  perhaps that could be brought into play  again to try and get boris johnson out  out of power  that might be by the by though because  if we see more cabinet resonation  resignations his position may become  untenable anyway  well we challenged in london on a  dramatic day in british politics okay  well let's uh bring in uh sir john  curtis now who's the politics professor  at the university of strathclyde he  joins us from the french village of  mosse thank you so much for your time  sir john um  is it possible to say is this the  beginning of the end for boris johnson  if we look at the numbers  well it certainly looks as though it's  going to be difficult for him probably  the crucial thing now is even if no  other cabinet minister resigns what  happens  in the election that's due to take place  i think it's next week uh for the  officers of the 1922 committee that's  the  uh  the organization that represents  conservative backed ventures  there was already expected to be an  attempt to put in place in those  positions  those who would be willing to  contemplate holding another vote in less  than the 12 months you've just been  talking about  there must now be an increased chance  that an attempt to fill those posts with  those who don't want boris johnson will  succeed  and there must now have been increased  chances if there is another ballot  the second time around boris johnson  will not have  the majority behind him because  certainly i think the resignations of  rishi sunak and of sasha javid is going  to take some of the back benches and  perhaps in private other ministers  with them so  boris johnson is we know is very very  unlikely to resign he will attempt to  fill the two vectors that exist and the  fact that liz trusts the foreign  secretary has decided to stand by him  probably means he's at least okay for  the next 24 hours but  it's pretty clear that those mps who  were disenchanted with boris johnson two  weeks ago  or even more disenchanted now and the  question is to what extent do those two  cabinet resolution designations  act as a litmus test of increased  concern amongst mps who two weeks ago  were willing to give them the benefit of  the dart but perhaps perhaps are no  longer willing to do so  this 