s etc  and just to give you one indication this  afternoon that i picked up that perhaps  tory mps are no longer willing to back  boris johnson just speaking to a local  radio bbc station based in coventry  they'd rang around all their  conservative mps trying to get one of  them to comment and none of them would  come on air and in the end it's the  question of whether or not tory mps  either because of an actual concern or  because of basically ethical concerns  about the prime minister whether they're  willing to defend him or not and the way  in which some ministers were  left to try to defend the prime minister  in the last 72 hours only to discover  that the  defense that they would give and prove  not to be the truth he's not going to  encourage other conservative mps uh in  uh any continued willingness to stand by  the prime minister now clearly  conservative mps are very much  keen to to hold on to their jobs going  forward but do you think all of this all  of these  shenanigans these ups and downs by the  prime minister have dealt a damaging  blow to the wider conservative party  well  i'm not sure that this story is going to  do any more damage to the conservatives  than particularly has already done i  mean the reason why this story has  lengths is because of the way in which  as you were discussing it it echoes the  concerns about the prime minister that  the owen paterson affair and the  partygate affair uh already had and the  partygate affair either isn't a  westminster bubble affair this is  something that touched a lot of people  emotionally because they knew what they  had to do during the lockdown the  sacrifices they made some of them deeply  personal ones and then they discovered  that in their view the prime minister  wasn't wasn't keeping to it that's where  the damage arose because in the wake of  that  people were saying hey  do i trust this gentleman's judgment and  b and crucially  do i believe what he says anymore 75  percent of the public have been saying  for weeks they do not believe what the  prime minister has been saying about  party gates and the question that's been  facing the conservative mps ever since  that is well they don't believe the  prime minister over partygate will they  believe him over the cost of living  crisis the ukraine crisis and the other  very very substantial challenges that  face this government if the prime  minister's no longer got the cr  credibility in the eyes of a significant  section of the public then recovery from  the party's current position under his  leadership was going to be difficult now  maybe we are not going to find out  whether you can turn things around but  that certainly was a big question  hanging over whether or not conservative  mps would be wise in uh keeping him as  their leader  sir john curtis political professor at  the university of strathclyde i'm sure  you'll be watching these political  developments very closely thank you  you're welcome

